---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/apps/sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/sendlane');

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Can a small Shopify store justify Sendlane?',
    answer: 'Compare the actual send workload and support scope with your budget. Sendlane advertises send-based pricing with unlimited contacts; order count is not its pricing unit. A small program may value assisted onboarding, but request the deliverables and recurring commitment before deciding. No universal order threshold establishes value.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Sendlane migration from Klaviyo actually work on Shopify?',
    answer: 'Request a current migration scope covering event mapping, flow rebuilds, suppression history, asset rights and access. Validate with test recipients and a staged cutover: one sender per journey, old queued jobs cancelled, replayed events deduplicated. Set acceptance criteria from eligibility and reconciliation, not a fixed deadline or revenue tolerance.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who owns the program after Sendlane onboarding?',
    answer: 'The merchant should retain account administration, billing access, DNS control, creative rights and a named retention owner. Ask the onboarding team to demonstrate a flow change and rollback with your operator present. Support can help with implementation; it does not choose your offers or approve your audience.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What Shopify events does Sendlane sync for lifecycle automation?',
    answer: 'Ask for a current demonstration of the exact Shopify and subscription events your flows require, including payload fields, identity mapping, latency, replay behavior and plan limits. Test purchase exits and missing data. This page does not certify event coverage or comparative segmentation depth.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Sendlane SMS good enough to skip Postscript on Shopify?',
    answer: 'Evaluate the actual SMS route, destination rates, replies, sender registration, channel-specific permission and suppression propagation. Confirm plan scope and reply staffing with each provider. Order volume alone cannot establish adequacy, legal compliance or a specialist advantage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do some merchants reject Sendlane after getting a quote?',
    answer: 'A quote can be rejected when its matched all-in cost exceeds the value of the contracted work. Compare sends, onboarding, integration, ongoing internal reviews, reply labor and exit costs over the same period. No merchant quote or observed rejection is established here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What deliverability tooling does Sendlane include?',
    answer: 'Confirm current monitoring, authentication review and support deliverables in the proposed plan. Verify domain authentication, permission quality, bounces, complaints and suppression behavior independently. Support cannot guarantee inbox placement or cure an ineligible audience.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When should a happy Klaviyo store stay put instead of switching to Sendlane?',
    answer: 'Keep an existing system when it demonstrably meets required workflows and migration risk exceeds a credible benefit. Compare matched program costs and verified access, event and suppression requirements. Neither a feature label nor attributed revenue alone settles the decision.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the most common post-migration Sendlane failure mode?',
    answer: 'An ownership gap can leave eligibility, offers and queues stale after any migration. Assign an internal owner for approvals, current suppressions and purchase exits, testing and change reviews. No six-month revenue pattern or universal review deadline is established.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/apps" class="text-sm font-bold text-[#9a5b16] hover:text-[#24170f]">← All tool reviews</a>
      <p class="catalog-kicker mt-6">Guided ecommerce</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Evaluate Sendlane against the Shopify journeys and migration work your team actually needs. Request a current demonstration and a written onboarding scope, including approvals, flow rebuilds, eligibility and support. Do not assume a package includes human migration work, a particular entry price or protection against revenue loss.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-[#5d3a1d]">
        Illustrative evaluation cases, not observed tests: a specialty tea brand planning a Klaviyo exit, a vinyl shop evaluating cart SMS and a climbing retailer comparing onboarding costs. Use each to define a test workload rather than a success story.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend review</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <TldrBox
      title="Verdict: Sendlane for Shopify"
      picks={[
        { label: 'Positioning', tool: 'Ecommerce-first', reason: 'Evaluate required DTC journeys with actual Shopify event inputs.' },
        { label: 'Support', tool: 'Migration help', reason: 'Confirm flow rebuilds, suppression validation and approvals in the written scope.' },
        { label: 'Channels', tool: 'Email + SMS', reason: 'Demonstrate current channel eligibility and reporting in the proposed plan.' },
        { label: 'Pricing', tool: 'Quote required', reason: 'Compare matched messaging, onboarding, integration and internal labor.' },
        { label: 'Best for', tool: 'Verified workload', reason: 'Brands investing in retention with migration needs and no dedicated Klaviyo ops lead.' },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Retention migration with a merchant-owned handoff</h2>
      <p>
        For a tea brand, compare replenishment event mapping and maintainable cohort rules. For vinyl, demonstrate variant-level eligibility and purchase exits; for climbing gear, test the exact fit attributes needed. Confirm each proposed provider route and plan rather than inferring capability, launch speed or suitability from order volume.
      </p>
      <p>A tea migration can preserve useful replenishment logic without preserving obsolete audience assumptions. Record each SKU family, qualifying order, subscription pause and subsequent purchase exit. Have the merchant reproduce one rule change after handoff. That demonstrates maintainability; it does not establish fee savings or payback.</p>
      <p>
        Request an itemized quote and a sample onboarding deliverable before committing time or money. Compare the same software, messaging, implementation and review workload across proposals. Written availability, access and commercial terms matter more than an assumed premium for hand-holding.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ledger-card mb-12">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Shopify integration depth</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Native events, catalog power, and where Sendlane stops</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Demonstrate the Shopify inputs required for each journey: cart, checkout, purchase, fulfillment and product data. Test payload fields, identity mapping, delays and missing events in the proposed integration. Reconcile attributed orders with store records using consistent windows and deduplication; dashboard agreement does not prove incremental revenue.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <strong>Capabilities to verify:</strong> Demonstrate the proposed email and SMS routes, SKU-level replenishment rules, testing controls and permission/suppression propagation. Confirm which migration and deliverability work is included, who performs it and what access is required.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <strong>Gaps versus Klaviyo:</strong> Test the same predictive inputs, browse identity, fit attributes and reusable flow logic in both proposed plans. Document actual missing fields or controls and the effort of a workaround; this review does not certify comparative exclusions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <strong>Operational gotchas:</strong> Monitor SMS cost by destination and message type, including reply staffing. Test guest identity and duplicate-event handling instead of assuming onboarding merges profiles. Verify subscription event mapping and current pause, purchase and suppression state before scheduling replenishment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Pricing breakdown with merchant math</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.sendlane.com/pricing">Sendlane pricing page</a> describes send-based pricing, unlimited contacts, forms, automation and Shopify integration. Its support tiers distinguish assisted onboarding from custom migration services, and dedicated services are tied to annual contracts. Obtain the applicable scope and peak-send overage terms in writing. SMS, implementation and internal review belong in the budget too. Reviewed November 25, 2026; no current dollar quote is asserted here.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario A — 720 orders/mo, 18k profiles, 42k emails/mo, 2,800 SMS/mo.</strong> Illustrative quote workload, not an observed sales call. Obtain comparable destination and message assumptions, software, onboarding and ongoing labor from each provider. Include reply staffing and implementation; no all-in vendor total or reporting advantage is established.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario B — Tea brand post-Klaviyo, 22k profiles, 55k emails/mo.</strong> Illustrative migration budget: compare matched software and messaging with flow rebuilds, integration, internal reviews and an ongoing retention owner. Include hire burden and creative work on both sides. No savings, included quarter of onboarding or agency advantage is established.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario C — 480 orders/mo climbing gear, price rejection.</strong> Illustrative greenfield evaluation: obtain matched proposals for welcome and cart journeys, including eligibility tests and staff time. A lower software quote may carry more internal work; decide from the actual scope rather than an invented rejection or order-volume rule.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">1. Klaviyo migration — three tea types, different replenishment windows</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> Illustrative tea migration. Establish reorder windows from actual product consumption and customer behavior, including subscription pause state, rather than assigning fixed days by tea type. <strong>Proposed onboarding test:</strong> Rebuild SKU-family eligibility, current suppression and purchase exits, documenting event inputs and owner in a merchant-controlled format with verified access rights.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Result:</strong> No migration result was observed. Proposed acceptance checks cover event completeness, eligible recipients, queue cancellation, deduplication and reconciled orders. Compare attribution windows explicitly and use a feasible control for incremental impact; an arbitrary revenue tolerance is not migration proof.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">2. Cart + SMS — vinyl record shop, 720 orders/mo</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> Illustrative vinyl cart evaluation. <strong>Proposed setup:</strong> Test limited-pressing availability and current purchase exits before any reminder. Select email and SMS touches from purpose/channel permission and a justified hypothesis, with a single sender per touch and cancellation of stale queued jobs. Do not assume a fixed eight-hour SMS delay is appropriate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Result:</strong> No recovery rate or provider advantage was observed. Measure incremental contribution using a feasible holdout, discount and return costs, destination fees and reply labor. Compare a unified route with a specialist using the same workload and operational controls.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">3. Price rejection — climbing gear at 480 orders/mo</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> Illustrative climbing-gear greenfield setup. Obtain current comparable quotes for cart and welcome, including flow QA, integration and internal review. <strong>Result:</strong> No selection, quoted saving or six-day launch is established; suitability depends on verified scope and staff capacity.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">4. Post-migration stagnation — the warning story</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> Illustrative home-fragrance handoff risk after a Mailchimp migration. Assign an owner for eligibility, offers and seasonal creative, with access and an agreed change-review cadence. <strong>Result:</strong> No revenue plateau or recovery was observed. Evaluate the handoff through maintainable documentation and tests, not a claimed quarterly rebound.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Pros</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          {app.strengths.map((s) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">+</span>{s}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Cons</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          {app.limits.map((l) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">−</span>{l}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Who should buy</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Verified lifecycle workload and a plausible contribution case for the quoted scope</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Mid-migration from Klaviyo or Mailchimp with broken flows</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Teams that can demonstrate coordinated email and SMS eligibility in the proposed route</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Finance can reconcile orders and distinguish attributed revenue from causal lift</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Who should skip</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Quoted commitment exceeds the program budget or required work</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Greenfield scope does not justify the proposed onboarding work</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Required predictive inputs or controls cannot be demonstrated</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Required API or operator workflow cannot be demonstrated</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Sendlane operating discipline on Shopify</h2>
      <p>
        Assign an internal retention owner before cutover to approve rebuilt flows, document event meanings and review current permissions, suppressions and purchases. Set a change-review cadence from program risk and staffing. Onboarding completion alone cannot establish growth, and an untouched flow does not prove a fixed revenue trajectory.
      </p>
      <p>
        Document suppression history, purpose/channel permission evidence and withdrawal state before migration. Verify what can be exported and retained, then test current recipient eligibility at dispatch. Assign sender and queue ownership across welcome, cart and winback, cancel stale jobs and deduplicate replayed events; a periodic audit alone does not prevent collisions.
      </p>
      <p>Compare a supported migration with a self-managed rebuild using the same acceptance cases. Include who reconnects capture forms, restores preferences, reviews creative and handles support after launch. A lower subscription can still require more internal work; neither a merchant category nor anxiety alone proves a support premium worthwhile.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Proposed Sendlane stack acceptance cases</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Specialty tea Klaviyo exit:</strong> Proposed stack test: validate subscription and purchase events, SKU-family reorder inputs and capture-source permission before welcome or replenishment. Verify any capture integration, current suppressions and shared queue ownership; no five-week delivery or revenue tolerance is established.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Vinyl 720 orders/mo:</strong> Proposed stack test: evaluate email and SMS as separate permitted channels with current stock and purchase state, a single sender per reminder and cancellation of old jobs. Compare destination costs and reply labor without assuming a recovery rate or a volume-based specialist cutoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Home fragrance post-Mailchimp:</strong> Proposed handoff test: ensure the merchant can maintain creative, segments, suppression and purchase exits with documented access and asset rights. Review observable maintenance and incremental contribution; no migration success or later revenue recovery was observed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Newsletters, lifecycle, and transactional coordination</h2>
      <p>
        For newsletters, lifecycle and service notices, assign purpose, recipient eligibility and sender ownership separately. Demonstrate the proposed route rather than assuming shared product blocks prevent collisions. Check current permission, suppression, purchase state and queued offers at dispatch; choose exclusions from the journey, not a blanket seventy-two-hour purchaser rule.
      </p>
      <p>
        Evaluate welcome by signup source, post-purchase education, SKU-family replenishment and winback against actual inputs and consumption evidence. Confirm what onboarding rebuilds. Identify the sender of confirmations and shipping updates separately from promotional journeys, with current suppression propagation and cancellation rules; a tag alone does not establish coordination.
      </p>
      <p>For limited pressings, test variant availability immediately before dispatch and remove sold-out offers from queued campaigns. For tea, check replenishment after a repeat purchase or subscription pause. For climbing gear, demonstrate the exact fit fields before promising tailored recommendations. These cases identify data requirements without asserting a segmentation winner.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ledger-card mb-12">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Migration checklist</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Sendlane switch operating contract</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Export segment definitions and document boolean logic before cutover — agree which flows onboarding rebuilds and retain their definitions with merchant access</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Validate with test recipients, then cut over one sender per journey; cancel old queued jobs and reconcile eligibility and orders</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Validate permission by purpose and channel, withdrawals and current suppressions; confirm applicable obligations separately</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Assign an internal retention owner before kickoff; set change reviews from workload and journey risk</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Choose cutover timing from data readiness, staff coverage and rollback capacity around peak demand</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="card mb-12">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Compare alternatives</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Sendlane vs other Shopify email tools</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-lg font-bold">
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> — data depth vs SMB speed</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a> — compare operator workflows and implementation scope</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart guide</a> — cart recovery execution detail</li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment guide</a> — reorder window logic</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- EXPANSION_BLOCK_v2 -->

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Sendlane in a three-layer Shopify stack</h2>
      <p>
        Sendlane is a candidate when the verified platform and onboarding scope address an identified retention workload. Order volume alone does not establish value; compare matched all-in costs and operational requirements.
      </p>
      <p>Capture continuity deserves its own acceptance case. Inventory every popup, footer form, checkout checkbox and external landing page, including its source identifier, offer and destination list. Test a new subscriber, a returning subscriber and a withdrawn subscriber through each active route. A successful import does not prove that tomorrow’s signups reach the right welcome path. Keep service notices assigned to their actual sender while changing promotional flows.</p>
      
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="ledger-card">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Evaluation checklist</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Score Sendlane on your Shopify store</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Can a new signup be traced from each live form to the correct welcome offer, including a resubscription?</li>
        
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Can the merchant change, pause and restore a replenishment branch without the migration partner?</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Who can stop each sender if a withdrawn subscriber reappears through an old capture integration?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- EXPANSION_BLOCK_v3 -->

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Sendlane migration and co-existence</h2>
      <p>Treat account continuity as a release dependency. Confirm merchant-controlled admin and billing access, sender-domain records, integration credentials and a contact for urgent support. Export the agreed flow definitions and audience rules before removing old access. Run a merchant-led edit, pause and rollback exercise; this is a proposed drill, not an observed reduction in operating time.</p>
      <p>
        Use a staged migration with test recipients and one live sender per journey. Cancel old queued jobs, deduplicate replayed events and reconcile audience eligibility and orders before expanding. Set timing from data readiness, staffing and rollback capacity rather than a two-week minimum, fixed holdout or attribution tolerance.
      </p>
      <p>
        When proposing specialist SMS, capture or shipping integrations, verify the route and permission data in each account. Document purpose/channel eligibility, priority, current suppressions and purchases, queue cancellation and replay deduplication. Name an owner who can disable each sender and monitor replies.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-8">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold">
      <a class="link" href="/apps">All Shopify email tools →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparison hub →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/for/food-and-beverage">Food & beverage stores →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sendlane Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
